`running_sum`: build a fresh vector and `push_back` one value per input element. `out.reserve(v.size())` first so it allocates once instead of growing.

```cpp
std::vector<int> out;
out.reserve(v.size());
int acc = 0;
for (int x : v) out.push_back(acc += x);
return out;
```

The starter's `get_at` reads `v[i]` without checking. `operator[]` never checks, so `i == v.size()` reads memory past the end of the buffer (ASan calls it heap-buffer-overflow). Check first:

```cpp
if (i >= v.size()) return false;
out = v[i];
return true;
```
